fs/pstore: don't block in atomic context

★ STATUS: INERT IN THE SHIPPED BUILD, DELIBERATELY RETAINED.

The DIR-842 subtarget sets `# CONFIG_PSTORE is not set`, so `fs/pstore/` is not
compiled and this patch changes nothing in the image. It is kept because the
bug it fixes is real, was found on this hardware, and is not obvious: anyone who
re-enables pstore (see docs/COLD-BOOT-TX-WEDGE.md §9.8 for the experiment that
would justify doing so) needs it, and rediscovering it costs a hang that looks
like a dead box rather than a crash.

Why pstore is off at all: an attached ramoops wedges CPU-originated TX on this
board. RX stays healthy while everything the box originates is dropped. The
mechanism is NOT established -- see docs/COLD-BOOT-TX-WEDGE.md §9.6-9.8, which
retracts the earlier "ramoops corrupts DMA memory" explanation.

--- a/fs/pstore/platform.c
+++ b/fs/pstore/platform.c
@@ -140,6 +140,20 @@
 	if (in_nmi())
 		return true;

+	/*
+	 * A dump reaching here with reason == KMSG_DUMP_OOPS can still be
+	 * running in interrupt or otherwise atomic context: die() only
+	 * escalates to panic() for the fault that is actually unwinding, so
+	 * a second, re-entrant dump racing the first one's held buf_lock
+	 * keeps the OOPS reason even though we are not in a safe context to
+	 * block. down_interruptible() below calls schedule(), which is
+	 * fatal on its own ("bad: scheduling from the idle thread!") when
+	 * called from there, turning what would have been a clean
+	 * panic_on_oops reboot into a permanent hang.
+	 */
+	if (in_interrupt() || in_atomic())
+		return true;
+
 	switch (reason) {
 	/* In panic case, other cpus are stopped by smp_send_stop(). */
 	case KMSG_DUMP_PANIC:
